al boundaries."}, {"sourceSlug": "the-beatles", "sourceName": "The Beatles", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "psychedelic-rock", "targetName": "Psychedelic Rock", "context": "The Beatles created iconic psychedelic rock music that helped launch the genre into mainstream popularity."}], "places": [{"name": "San Francisco, United States", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "London, United Kingdom", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Woodstock, United States", "role": "Major Festival"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: psychedelic rock", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "psychedelic-rock"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q206159", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychedelic_rock"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "psychedelic rock contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Psychedelic rock significantly influenced popular culture and music, shaping the trajectory of artistic expression in the late 20th century and beyond, as evidenced by its lasting impact on genres and music festivals.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-26T01:06:59Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30182278383", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Psychedelic rock is a subgenre of rock music that originally emerged during the mid-1960s, inspired by psychedelic culture and primarily centered around the influence of psychoactive and hallucinogenic drugs. The music incorporated new electronic sound effects and recording techniques, extended instrumental solos, and improvisation. Many psychedelic groups differ in style, with the label often applied spuriously.", "newValue": "Psychedelic rock emerged as a distinctive subgenre of rock music in the mid-1960s, primarily in the United States and the United Kingdom, as an artistic response to the burgeoning counterculture movement. This genre played a pivotal role in shaping the cultural landscape of the time, influencing not only music but also visual arts and social movements. Key figures such as Jimi Hendrix, The Beatles, and Pink Floyd were instrumental in popularizing this genre, each contributing unique elements that defined the sound and aesthetic of psychedelic rock.

The movement reached its zenith between 1965 and 1970, characterized by innovative use of technology and sound. The Beatles' 1967 album "Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band" is often credited with epitomizing the genre, utilizing studio techniques like tape loops and backward recording that revolutionized music production. Other notable albums from this era include The Byrds' "Mr. Tambourine Man" and Jefferson Airplane's "Surrealistic Pillow," each blending rock with folk, blues, and avant-garde influences. The music often served as a soundtrack to social and political upheaval, with songs addressing themes of peace, love, and rebellion.

The legacy of psychedelic rock endures in contemporary music, influencing genres such as progressive rock, grunge, and even electronic music. The counterculture ideals of the 1960s, particularly the exploration of consciousness and alternative lifestyles, found new expression in later musical movements. Psychedelic rock also contributed to the rise of music festivals, such as Woodstock in 1969, which became symbols of peace and unity. As Jimi Hendrix famously declared, "Music doesn't lie. If there is something to be changed in this world, then it can only happen through music.""}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-26T01:06:59Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30182278383", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Cultural, artistic, or intellectual creation"], "newValue": ["The rise of the counterculture movement in the 1960s fostered an environment ripe for artistic experimentation.", "The availability and popularization of psychoactive substances, such as LSD, influenced the themes and sounds of the music.", "Technological advancements in recording and amplification allowed for innovative soundscapes and production techniques."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-26T01:06:59Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30182278383", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission"], "newValue": ["Psychedelic rock contributed to the normalization of drug culture and its association with artistic expression.", "The genre inspired the creation of music festivals, which became cultural phenomena celebrating peace and community.", "Psychedelic rock influenced future musical genres, such as progressive rock and electronic music, shaping the landscape of popular music."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-26T01:06:59Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30182278383", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "psychedelic-rock", "sourceName": "psychedelic rock", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "era-contemporary", "t